## Why This Is Asked
Workday needs engineers who can design scalable operators that handle enterprise-scale workloads without performance degradation or resource leaks.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidates will discuss: controller-runtime patterns, workqueue management, finalizers for cleanup, watch bookmarks for efficiency, backoff strategies, resource quotas, and proper event filtering to reduce reconciliation noise.

## Code Example
```typescript
func (r *MicroserviceReconciler) Reconcile(ctx context.Context, req ctrl.Request) (ctrl.Result, error) {
    // Use workqueue with rate limiting
    if !r.queue.CanAdd() {
        return ctrl.Result{RequeueAfter: time.Second * 5}, nil
    }

    // Implement finalizer pattern
    if !microservice.ObjectMeta.DeletionTimestamp.IsZero() {
        if containsString(microservice.ObjectMeta.Finalizers, myFinalizerName) {
            // Cleanup logic
            return r.cleanup(ctx, microservice)
        }
        return ctrl.Result{}, nil
    }

    // Efficient reconciliation with selective updates
    return r.reconcileWithSelectiveUpdate(ctx, microservice)
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a scalable governance mechanism spanning policy storage, reconciliation, and enforcement across many namespaces, while preserving auditability and rollback.

## Key Concepts

- Namespace-scoped policy governance
- Central policy store with versioning
- Auto-generation of RBAC Roles, ResourceQuota, and LimitRange
- Real-time enforcement via admission webhook
- Drift detection and safe rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: reconcile NamespaceGuardPolicy and apply RBAC/limits across a namespace
async function reconcile(ns) {
  const policy = fetchPolicy(ns);
  if (!policy) return;
  ensureRBAC(ns, policy.rbac);
  ensureQuota(ns, policy.quota);
  ensureLimits(ns, policy.limits);
  audit(ns, policy.version);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this at scale across thousands of namespaces?
- How would you handle policy propagation latency and eventual consistency?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design a Kubernetes-native multi-tenant shard architecture, implement operator-driven lifecycle management, and construct safe upgrade and backup workflows—essential skills for building scalable platforms serving multiple customers.

## Key Concepts

- CustomResourceDefinition design and operator reconciliation loops
- StatefulSets with per-tenant PVCs and resource quotas
- Canary rollouts with per-tenant rate limiting
- Cross-tenant backup orchestration and disaster recovery
- PodDisruptionBudgets for controlling upgrade blast radius

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo reconciliation loop
async function reconcileTenantShard(cr) {
  const ns = cr.metadata.namespace;
  await ensureNamespace(ns);
  const sts = buildStatefulSet(cr);
  await applyStatefulSet(sts);
  const pdb = buildPodDisruptionBudget(cr);
  await applyPDB(pdb);
  const backupJob = buildBackupJob(cr);
  await scheduleBackup(backupJob);
  await updateStatus(cr, { phase: 'Ready' });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes the ability to design a robust cross-namespace orchestration primitive, with DAG scheduling, idempotent reconciliation, and cleanup semantics. It also touches RBAC, metrics, and testing at scale.

## Key Concepts

- CustomResourceDefinition and controller patterns
- DAG scheduling and dependency resolution
- Per-namespace isolation and quotas
- Idempotent reconciler design
- Finalizers and cleanup lifecycle
- Observability: conditions, events, metrics across namespaces
- Backoff strategies with jitter

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo reconciler structure
async function reconcile(dataMigrationPlan) {
  // Parse DAG and identify ready tasks
  const readyTasks = parseDAG(dataMigrationPlan.spec.tasks);

  for (const task of readyTasks) {
    // Check idempotency
    if (await jobExists(task.name)) {
      continue;
    }

    // Create Job with backoff strategy
    await createJob(task);

    // Add finalizer for cleanup
    await addFinalizer(dataMigrationPlan, task.name);
  }

  // Update status and conditions
  await updateStatus(dataMigrationPlan);
}
```

